{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["When you besiege a city many days — and you need to cut down trees to build mantlets, a palisade, and siege-ramps." "For from it you may eat — since you need them for food, after you capture the city and it becomes yours." "And it you shall not cut down — that food tree which is not a strength and fortress for the men of the city, such as the distant ones — those you shall not cut down." "For the man is a tree of the field, to come before you in the siege — every 'ki' that follows is explicated only as: ״אותו לא תכרות״ — you shall not cut that down, but ״עץ השדה לבא — האדם — מפניך במצור״ — that tree you shall cut down. These are the ones near the city in which the men of the city hide, who flee before you and come into the city, as written: ״ותבא העיר במצור״." "For the man — rather, 'the man is a tree of the field' — rather, the tree of the field brings the man to come before you in the siege, for it causes the man to come before you in the siege."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {3 "Rashbam reads the difficult phrase by repunctuating: only trees that shelter fleeing defenders may be cut down. The quotation ותבא העיר במצור appears to be from Ezekiel 4:3 (ואתה תן פניך אליהם ותהיה במצור וצרת אותה), or possibly an abbreviated citation of a phrase describing a city under siege.", 4 "The lemma appears to reflect the unpointed consonantal text אך עץ אשר תדע כי לא עץ מאכל הוא; the first 'כי האדם' is the lemma from v. 19, the second is Rashbam's re-reading."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:39:20.263854461Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}